# 4장 (4판) 영어판: 퀘스트 ID -> (제목, 받을 때, 진행 중, 끝낼 때)
EN = {
"story_4_01": ("Inspecting the Seals",
 "Sit. I spent the night over Cornelia's map. Six of the seven seals are marked on it. The seventh lies at the very bottom of hell, and even the captain couldn't draw it. Each of the six holds an eye, and at each eye a guardian is awake. You saw it at Roran's old expedition sites: the seals are rotting. A rotten seal bursts. Like the captain's table on that ship. Before they burst, each must be put to rest inside a Gate.",
 "Hear Elder Erwin out.",
 "Keep the eyes yourselves. I only need to see them. Truly. What use would an old man have for such things? The first place is the Soul Forge in hell. Take Bram. Nobody hears what iron says better than he does. (The Elder straightens his glove. He seems in unusually good spirits today.)"),
"story_4_02": ("Barred from the Outside",
 "The Soul Forge was the underground smithy of the Caldera kingdom. Bragan, King of the Furnace, took the first eye and buried it there. Its guardian is a giant the smiths forged. They say the hammering has not stopped in a thousand years. Look at the door first, with Bram. I'll wait outside it.",
 "Find the Soul Forge in hell, examine the door with Bram, and put 2 ignited revenants to rest.",
 "Barred from the outside, you say. Yes. That was Bragan. He left a hundred smiths inside and barred the door. So the way to make the giant would never get out. The king wrote that he had 'entrusted' it to them. …The revenants were still standing at their anvils, you say. The order was never finished, so they can't leave. And there is no door to leave by."),
"story_4_03": ("The Cooling Furnace",
 "The great furnace in there is half dead. The giant lives on that fire, and with the fire weak it grows uneasy and hammers all the harder. If we mean to put it to rest inside a Gate, the furnace has to be revived first. Bring me beacon cores and netherite scrap. One more thing. I want to heat the expedition's blades in that furnace, once each. Only steel tempered in that fire will bite the giant's hide.",
 "Feed 4 blaze rods into the great furnace of the Soul Forge to wake its fire, and bring 2 netherite scrap to Bram the smith.",
 "It's alive. See how the light's changed. (Bram puts the expedition's blades into the furnace one by one and draws them out.) Done. But when the furnace came back, an iron plate showed under the anvil. An order sheet. What the king ordered. I can read, but not the old script. Go and read it. And about that hook with no name. …No, read the order first, then we'll talk."),
"story_4_04": ("The Order for a Giant",
 "Read me that order. A smith looks at an order and knows what the thing was born to do. Know that, then put it to rest. Berserkers circle the furnace. Fiercer than the revenants. Take care.",
 "Examine the order sheet under the anvil in the Soul Forge, and put 2 ignited berserkers to rest.",
 "'Give it no name.' So it has none. With no name there is nothing for the fire to eat, and that's how it lasted a thousand years. And the last line. 'See that it cannot strike the door.' …But it does. With its fists. For a thousand years. Though its own body was forged not to. It's trying to let the hundred out. The hands that made it. The nameless hook is the hundredth smith's place. The one who never carved a name on anything. There's a saying in my family. 'The night the door was barred, the hundredth passed a handful of caged fire out through the gap. And stayed.' The one who took it, outside the door, was our founder."),
"story_4_05": ("The Son Without a Name",
 "You read the order. Yes, that was the kind of king Bragan was. He loved skill and did not trust those who had it. Open the Memory Gate. The giant must be put to rest inside. When the hammer comes down, don't hide, roll aside. And do not hate it. All it has done for a thousand years is knock on a door. Asking to be let out.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Netherite Monstrosity to rest.",
 "As it fell it reached for the door one last time, you say. …Well done. Truly well done. Have Isabel write the name. It has none? Then have her write that. 'The son who was given no name.' With the names of the ninety-nine, and a place for the one without. I've left the door open. Even an old man's strength was enough to draw the bar. A thousand years rots lead too. Turn in early tonight, you too. A night after the knocking stops is uncommonly quiet."),
"story_4_06": ("Portraits of the Elders",
 "Wait a moment, please. This isn't about the seals. While writing down the forge's names I looked up the Order's history. The portraits of the past Elders hang in the gallery. Twenty-nine of them. I walk past every day and I've never really looked. Today I looked. Please look with me, just once. Tell me if I'm seeing things.",
 "Examine the portraits of the past Elders with Isabel the archivist.",
 "I'm not seeing things, am I. All twenty-nine wear gloves. All with the left hand laid over the right. And every painter is different. And on the back of the first Elder's portrait the painter wrote: 'Told not to paint the hands.' …It could be a family custom. Manners handed down. I'm going to think of it that way. For now. The next seal is the desert, isn't it. I'll come too. There will be a lot to read."),
"story_4_07": ("Where the Water Goes",
 "Back in the desert, water rose where Sirok fell. It hasn't dried up. It's running down the thousand-year-old canal. The villagers told me. It flows only one way. Toward the king's garden. That's the second mark on the map. The Cursed Pyramid. Let's follow the water. Where it stops, that's where the king is waiting.",
 "Find the Cursed Pyramid in the desert, examine the dry basin with Isabel, put 4 koboletons to rest, then examine a fallen gardener.",
 "The water stopped at the door. A hand's width short. And the basin is dry. That's the last stretch Sirok never dug. And the koboletons. There were water-yoke marks on their bones. They were gardeners. They followed the king in and never came out. There's writing on the door. A lot of it. Before I read, let's look at the canal. There were stones standing along it on the way. Half buried in sand. Markers someone raised."),
"story_4_08": ("The Last Hand's Width",
 "I've read the whole door. It's the king's writing. Written from inside the garden, on the door. For someone outside to read. Did you bring the water? Fill the basin while you listen. We'll bridge the last hand's width. In Sirok's place.",
 "Pour 3 water buckets into the basin before the Cursed Pyramid's door, and hear the king's words from Isabel.",
 "The basin's full. Something stirred behind the door. Something very large. I heard bone on bone. It's the gatekeeper. The beast the king rode. He knew the price of the eye was a kingdom buried. So he walked into the garden himself and had the door shut, so the sand would swallow the garden before the villages. He saved his people. But he never told his two beasts. That he wasn't coming back. He only said 'wait'. That's what the king handed off. The telling of the truth."),
"story_4_09": ("The Sealed Arena",
 "I followed you late, and Isabel is shut inside. When the gatekeeper rose, every passage in the pyramid shifted, they say. Sand came pouring down. You can hear her through the wall. (Isabel's voice comes through the wall.) \"I… I'm trapped. Where I am looks like an arena. It's where they tested who could enter the garden. It says so on the wall. And one gladiator is still standing. It's looking at me. I think it's asking whether I'm worthy. All I have is a pen.\" …Hurry. That child doesn't know how to fight with a pen.",
 "Put the Kobolediator in the Cursed Pyramid to rest and rescue Isabel the archivist.",
 "(Isabel brushes off the sand and laughs. Her hands are shaking.) Thank you. That gladiator. It knelt before it fell. Not the way the loser kneels. The way the one who lets you pass kneels. It means we were worthy. The first to pass in a thousand years. There was a space on the wall for the names of those who passed. It was empty. I wrote one in. The expedition."),
"story_4_10": ("The Gatekeeper",
 "…The King of the Desert was a friend of my teacher's. No. So I've read. In books. The gatekeeper is the beast the king rode. They call it the Ancient Remnant. Nothing left but bone, and still it kept the door. Open the Memory Gate. Keep clear of where the sand heaves. It hides underground.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Ancient Remnant to rest.",
 "(The Elder says nothing for a long time.) The beast bowed its head as it fell, you say. As if greeting someone. The door opened and the king was sitting inside the garden. By the dry fountain. Facing the door. In his bones. …So he waited too. Knowing it wouldn't open. That was not in the books. Have Isabel write it down. I… am going to walk a while. Alone."),
"story_4_11": ("The Council of Eight Kings",
 "The third floor of the Spiral Abyss has opened. The Order's old records call it only 'the council'. The council a thousand years ago where they agreed to divide the Flame. The Chronicle says seven kings sat. The page we found early on said 'the eighth seat is empty; the seven emptied it'. Please see for yourself. How many seats there are.",
 "Clear the third floor of the Spiral Abyss.",
 "Eight, then. And the eighth king objected. 'A fire divided will one day be gathered again.' And the youngest king, shaking, said what? 'Feed it a prisoner's name.' …What does feeding a name mean? Dividing the Flame needed a name? And beside the eighth king stood a young apprentice in a grey cloak. And the memory broke off there. As if someone cut it. It's what my mother's echo said. 'When all are gathered, someone will use it.'"),
"story_4_12": ("One Minute To",
 "The third place is the city of Ordel, Queen of Cogs. Only the factory remains. The queen built a machine to reckon what was to come. And when she saw its answer she stopped every clock in the city. Go and see why. This time I'll come inside. I must see with my own eyes what that machine reckoned.",
 "Find the Ancient Factory, examine the great clock with Erwin, and stop 3 watchers.",
 "One minute to twelve. Every clock in the city. The queen asked the machine, 'When is the end?' The machine answered, and she stopped the clocks so that hour would never come. If the clocks don't run, the end doesn't arrive. A child's idea. …I know that feeling. The watchers are clock-keepers. They watch for anyone who winds a spring. What comes when one is wound, you'll soon learn."),
"story_4_13": ("The One Who Winds",
 "The machine is asleep. The city's hour stopped, so the machine stopped. To wake it the great clock has to run again. Bring a clock and redstone. I'll do the winding. When it's wound, the hunter will come. The Prowler. A machine that seeks whoever winds a spring. It will chase me. You strike it from behind while it does. …An old man playing bait. What next.",
 "Bring 1 clock and 8 redstone dust, pull the stake from the great clock, then stop the Prowler in the Ancient Factory.",
 "(The Elder catches his breath. He laughs.) Running, at my age. Roran would have teased me. The clock is going. Do you hear it? One minute has passed, for the first time in a thousand years. It's twelve. The machine has opened its eye. It has begun spitting paper. Everything it reckoned. A thousand years' worth at once. Your names are in it."),
"story_4_14": ("The Machine That Saw the End",
 "The Harbinger. A machine built to see the end. The queen couldn't bear the answer to her own question, so she handed it to the machine. 'You saw it, so you guard it.' Open the Memory Gate. Whatever reckoning it recites as it falls, let it pass. A machine only says what it saw. It doesn't know what it means.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Harbinger to rest.",
 "'Those who open the last door are the ones who hold the embers. The hand that guides them wears a glove.' That's what the machine recited. (The Elder strikes the floor with his staff. A face you have never seen on him.) What does a lump of iron know! A sum done a thousand years ago! People do not go the way they're reckoned. They do not. …Forgive me. I shouted. Patience wears thin with age. Give the printout to Isabel. The road you've walked is written on it. It reckoned even that."),
"story_4_15": ("The One Who Doesn't Go to Ask",
 "The machine said 'glove', I hear. The archivist brought me the printout. And Kael's letter. The one she tucked into the Chronicle back then. There was a hidden line. I know. I saw it that day. I pretended not to. Sit down. I'll tell you why a captain doesn't go and demand answers.",
 "Hear Captain Roran out.",
 "Go at him without proof and the Elder smiles and that's the end of it. And from then on he's more careful. I learned that twenty years ago. Hammer on a door in a hurry and all you do is ready whoever's behind it. Four seals left. Walk beside him to the end. Watch as you walk. A man who walks long enough starts to limp. On the side he's hiding. I can't go, with this leg. So I'm asking you. Be my eyes."),
"story_4_16": ("Inside the Stand",
 "Mia was polishing the Rune Altar and found the base plate of the stand is loose. Going by the drawing from the captain's cabin, the plate was always meant to open. There's writing inside, she says. Mirror writing. She read it and won't tell me. 'Look for yourself, teacher.' Please look with me. And while you're at the altar, watch who comes by.",
 "Examine the inside of the Rune Altar's stand.",
 "'Do not show these letters to that child.' …Who is 'that child'? We learned from that blueprint that the five stones are letters. Who are they hidden from? You saw the Elder pass just now. He stopped at the altar. He looked at the five stones a long time. Then he said, 'I feel I've seen this hand somewhere.' And walked on. He can't read them. Right in front of him. Mia cried when she saw it. She says she doesn't know why."),
"story_4_17": ("The Song That Never Stops",
 "The fourth place is under the sea, they say. I'll go. Since the thaw, singing comes up from below every night. Not that bait-voice from the ice maze. People singing. Many. Without rest. But these last days they drop out one by one. Fewer voices. Let's go see. The Elder says he'll stay ashore. He won't board a boat.",
 "Find the Sunken City, examine the slab of the song with Oscar, and defeat 4 deeplings.",
 "A lullaby. Sung for a thousand years. The queen told the priests, 'Don't stop. If you stop, it wakes.' No relief. When a priest fell, the child took it up. That's what the queen handed off. The lullaby she should have sung herself. …But why fewer voices? Something lay under the slab. A grey coin. Off a priest's neck. Someone paid the priests. To stop singing."),
"story_4_18": ("Those Who Would Cut the Song",
 "Two priests left. Two of them singing day and night. Their voices are worn to nothing. And big brutes and spell-casters stand round them. Not guarding. Trying to stop their mouths. Those ones wear the coins too. Clear them off. Don't touch the priests. Those two are the last line of a thousand years of lullaby.",
 "Defeat 2 deepling brutes and 2 deepling warlocks in the Sunken City.",
 "Too late. We cleared them, but one priest dropped in the gap. Worn out. The last one sings alone and hasn't the breath. There are breaks between the bars. Below, something big rolled over. It's waking. Before its time. …The last priest pressed something into my hand. An eye. The Eye of the Abyss. It means: we can't sing any more, put it to rest. In place of the lullaby."),
"story_4_19": ("The End of the Lullaby",
 "The Leviathan. A beast the queen raised, they say. The slab said that before the seal the children of the city under the sea rode on its back. The queen buried the eye inside it, and since the city would fall if it woke, she put it to sleep. A thousand years. Open the Memory Gate. Out of water it has no strength. Draw it to the shallows. I'll be at the door singing that song. I've got the eight bars by heart.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Leviathan to rest.",
 "It closed its eyes. Not sleeping eyes. Resting ones. The last priest stopped singing. First time in a thousand years. Know the first thing that priest said in a speaking voice? 'It's quiet.' (Oscar takes off his cap.) I went to tell the Elder and he wasn't on the shore. He was over the hill. Where you can't see the sea. With his back turned. I said, 'The sea's gone quiet.' He said, 'Has it. I didn't see.' Wouldn't see, not couldn't."),
"story_4_20": ("The Amethyst Lens",
 "The fifth place is the void. The land where you put the dragon to rest. There's a fallen citadel there. Where the eight kings held council. The void's mist hides it from the naked eye. Grind a lens from the shell of the great crab that lives in the amethyst nest underground and you can see through the mist. …I found that method when I was young. In a book. Yes, in a book.",
 "Find the amethyst nest underground and defeat the amethyst crab.",
 "It's cut well. (The Elder holds the lens to his eye. He looks a long time. The hand that lowers it is shaking.) I see it. The room that held eight thrones. You saw it in the Abyss, you said. The council. How far did you see? As far as the eighth standing up. …And nothing after. Yes. What came after, I too… it isn't in the books. Come. I'll go inside this time as well. This place I must go to."),
"story_4_21": ("Seven Seats",
 "The citadel. Look at the thrones one by one. They say that when the council ended each king left something on his seat. To use when they met again. They never met again. Endermaptera will have gathered. Clear them as you go.",
 "Find the Ruined Citadel in the End, examine the seven thrones with Erwin, and defeat 4 endermaptera.",
 "Seven things on seven seats. A hammer, a water jar, a winding key, a shell, a shard of ice, a lump of sealing wax, and an empty scabbard. That one is Caldern's. A scabbard and no sword. He gave the sword to his knight-commander. Along with the fire. Each of them left something. What remained after they'd handed off everything they could. The eighth seat is at the very back. Come. …No, wait. Let me get my breath."),
"story_4_22": ("The Eighth Seat",
 "I've come too. Captain Roran told you not to leave me alone, didn't he. I came so the Elder wouldn't be alone. Strange, isn't it. There's a golem standing before the eighth seat. Built of void stone. It isn't guarding a door. It's guarding the seat. So no one sits there. Put the golem to rest and let's look at the seat together. The Elder is back there. He can't come any closer.",
 "Put the Ender Golem in the Ruined Citadel to rest, and examine the eighth throne.",
 "The Elder's knees gave way in front of that seat. I held him up. His arm… was hot. Even through the glove. And he said, 'Don't read it. You won't be able to. Neither can I.' The scratched-out name. It was eight letters long. You can count them. The shapes are gone. But listen. The scrap of cloth on the back of the seat. It's grey. The hem of a cloak. A thousand years old and not rotted. No one was trying to sit here. Someone was hanging on here. Holding on to a person being dragged away."),
"story_4_23": ("Keeper of the Empty Throne",
 "…You saw me at my worst. Old knees. Nothing more. Put the Ender Guardian to rest. The keeper Velas set before his lock. To stop anyone who asks. Open the Memory Gate. The grey cloth? Old cloth. Nothing rots in the void. Nothing more than that. Hurry.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Ender Guardian to rest.",
 "As the Guardian fell it pointed at the eighth seat, you say. The thing set there to forbid asking pointed, at the last. (The Elder is silent a long while before he speaks.) It was under the wax, you say. That the question was sent to the scale. The Island of the Scale is not far. It was built apart from the citadel, so that judgment would not look at the kings' faces. If you want the answer, go. Isabel has already gone toward the bridge. I… do not set foot on that island. I will not step onto the scale."),
"story_4_24": ("A Prison Without Jailers",
 "The last place. The Frost Prison. The Elder truly didn't come. He only raised a hand at the Order's door. This king is unlike the others. The Chronicle says 'the King of Frost entrusted his eye to no one'. I took that as praise, but after seeing that small seat among the seven thrones I'm not sure. The seat with 'I'm afraid' scratched into it. Let's look at the prison first. At who locked up whom.",
 "Find the Frosted Prison, examine the cell, and put 4 draugr and 1 elite draugr to rest.",
 "Every cell door was locked from the inside. The keys are inside the cells. It's a prison with no jailers. The prisoner walked in and locked himself up. The draugr are the king's soldiers. He went in, so they followed. The king's writing was on the wall. An eighteen-year-old's hand. He keeps getting his spelling wrong. He wrote down, himself, what he said at the council."),
"story_4_25": ("The King's Guard",
 "The king's guard stands before his tomb chamber. A draugr wearing a crown, and an aptrgangr the size of a door. The crowned one is the king's brother. It was on the wall. 'My brother said he didn't want to be king. So I became king. He was sorry, and followed me in.' And listen. I'm done pretending not to know. The glove on the printout. The grey cloth. The portraits. My mother's last page. When we meet the king I'm going to ask. Who the grey cloak is. Someone who has lived a thousand years will know.",
 "Put the Royal Draugr and the Aptrgangr in the Frosted Prison to rest.",
 "The guard has fallen. The crown rolled and stopped at the chamber door. A voice is coming through the gap. It's young. About our Mia's age. It's calling, 'Brother?' …There's no one left to answer. We put him to rest. Please go and take what he says. I'll write it down. Don't open the door yet. He hasn't spoken with anyone before a fight in a thousand years."),
"story_4_26": ("Through the Door",
 "Please stand at the gap in the chamber door. The king will speak. Listen, and answer him. I'll write every word. And if the king asks you something… answer however you wish. There won't be a right answer.",
 "Hear the king through the gap in the tomb chamber door of the Frost Prison, and answer him.",
 "(Isabel's pen has stopped.) I have all of it. 'The grey cloak came a month ago too. In a thousand years he has come a hundred times. Each time his face was a little different. The glove was the same.' And the promise. 'A life without end.' The king was to take that and open the door. Give up the eye. But after your answer he was quiet a long time. Then he said, 'Then put me to rest. The promise was made with that man. You owe it nothing.' …The door opened from inside."),
"story_4_27": ("The King of Frost",
 "The king opened the door. Himself. Please open the Memory Gate. He must be put to rest inside so the seal doesn't burst. He flies through the air throwing spears and reaps the souls of the fallen. He'll fight as a fight is fought. A thousand years of habit. But he asked to be put to rest. Please don't forget that.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put Maledictus, King of Frost, to rest.",
 "What the king said as he fell. 'Tell the scholar in the grey cloak. I take back the promise. And that one did not blame you. I was there. I saw.' …I wrote it down. The king's name too. Maledictus. Eighteen years old. Someone was standing at the prison entrance. The one who said he couldn't come. Holding the door frame. Smoke was rising from his glove. He heard all of it. Then he turned and left. Toward the Order. Let's go. Now."),
"story_4_28": ("Under the Glove",
 "There's a light in the Elder's room. Let's go. I've brought everything I mean to ask about. The hands in the portraits. The hidden line in Kael's letter. The note from the captain's wallet. The machine's printout. The scrap of grey cloth. And my mother's last page. I'll lay them out. Please just stand beside me. He promised to answer when we came back and asked. The Elder doesn't break promises. That's what frightens me most.",
 "Go to Elder Erwin with Isabel the archivist.",
 "(The Elder takes off his glove. Slowly. One finger at a time. There is fire on the back of his right hand. Not burning. Carved. In the shape of a flame. No. Look again and there are strokes.) \"I told you to keep the eyes. They are husks. Six have closed, and that is enough. The rest is at the bottom of hell. I have waited a thousand years. Your expedition… you were good people. That was the hardest part.\" The Elder put his hand into the archive lantern. The fire passed into his hand. And he was gone."),
"story_4_29": ("The Lantern Gone Out",
 "…The archive lantern has gone out. My mother's lantern. The one we lit together when you first came. The Elder took it. I'm all right. Really. Only sit here with me a little while. You don't have to say anything.",
 "Stay with Isabel the archivist.",
 "My copybook was on the Elder's desk. From when I was seven. The one he corrected in red ink. He never threw it away. On the last page there was one line in his hand. 'This child will read to the end, like her mother. What do I do with her then?' …There was no answer written. I'm going to read to the end. And this time I'm going to write to the end. Captain Roran is gathering the expedition. They're going to the bottom of hell. Get some sleep before you go. I… don't think I'll manage any."),
"story_4_01a": ("Two Maps",
 "Before we leave, one moment. I want to lay the captain's map over the Order's wall map. She wrote a line at every mark. Short, like a clause of law. Read them with me. We should know where we're going.",
 "Lay the two maps over each other with Isabel in the archive.",
 "All six lines begin with 'those who went in'. The captain counted people, not places. And the Order's wall map has none of these six places. Not the map from a hundred years ago, not the one from three hundred. Yet the Elder said he saw the captain's map for the first time last night, and he never once asked the way. He read it in a book, I suppose. I'll ask which shelf when we're back. The first place is the forge. Bram is waiting."),
"story_4_01b": ("The First Page of the Ledger",
 "So you're going to the forge in hell, and the Elder said to take me. How did he know I know that door? Sit. I'll show you our family ledger. Eighteen generations of it. The first page isn't writing, it's a drawing. And help me pack. Hell's fire isn't like ours. Charcoal won't do; it has to be coal.",
 "Look at the ledger with Bram the smith, and bring 16 coal and 4 iron ingots.",
 "That'll do. The hammer hafts won't split in that heat. About the drawing. A barred iron door. A hand passing something through the gap. A hand receiving it. The receiving hand is our founder. Whose the giving hand is, nobody wrote. Grandfather only said 'go there and you'll know'. He never went. …Eighteen generations, and it's me who goes. My hands are shaking a little. They never shook in front of iron."),
"story_4_02a": ("A Hundred Hammer Pegs",
 "The hammers are lying where the revenants fell. They never let go. A thousand years. We can't just leave them. In a smithy you hang your hammer on your peg when the work is done. That's how you go home. Those people haven't gone home in a thousand years. Hang them with me. I'll read the name tags.",
 "Hang the hammers on their pegs with Bram in the Soul Forge. (Three rows)",
 "Ninety-nine, all hung. Every peg at a different height. Fitted to each one's reach. The left-handers' pegs bend the other way. That's how well they knew each other. …My family name isn't there. Not among the ninety-nine. Then who handed our founder the fire? The hundredth peg, the one with no tag. That's the only hammer I couldn't find. It isn't in here. Let's wake the furnace. More shows in good light."),
"story_4_04a": ("Three, a Pause, Two",
 "Let's ask the door whether the family story is true. Smiths have a knocking speech. You can't hear across a furnace, so you strike the anvil. 'Three, pause, two' means 'the fire is alive'. I thought only my family used it. If it came from here, it will understand. You strike. My hands can't be trusted just now.",
 "Knock on the inner door of the Soul Forge and listen for the answer.",
 "Did you hear? 'Two, pause, three.' That's 'open the door'. No, we don't use it that way at home. We use it for 'come and eat'. …So it was 'come and eat' all along. The hundredth taught it that. For a thousand years it's been knocking 'come and eat' at the door. Not to the ones inside. To the fire that was sent out. Asking if it's well. (Bram rests his forehead on the door a long while.) The Elder says it must still be put to rest. If the seal rots it bursts too. All right. Then while you fight, I'll keep knocking. Three, pause, two. So it doesn't fall asleep alone."),
"story_4_05a": ("The Hundredth Peg",
 "You're back. So am I. I brought one thing from the forge. The hundredth peg. Pulled it from the wall. It isn't stealing. Who else was going to bring it? It goes in my smithy wall. Bring me some iron. There's a peg and no hammer, so we forge one. You strike the first blow. The hand that put it to rest.",
 "Bring 3 iron ingots and hang the new hammer on the hundredth peg in Bram's smithy.",
 "It's hung. I carved no name. He wasn't one to carve his. Lorenzo came by and said he finally knows why I rap every piece of iron with my knuckles. A door-knocking family, he says. Nonsense. …But my grandfather did it too, and his father. See a thing, knock on it. Waiting for an answer. Eighteen generations of that. The first answer came today."),
"story_4_06a": ("The Far Chapter House",
 "Every brass plate says 'came from a far chapter house'. All twenty-nine. If I only knew where that house is, I'd have been worrying over nothing. I don't know the roads. Morris knows every road, and Haru keeps the record of who came to town and when. Ask them for me. I… can't yet ask the Elder to his face.",
 "Ask Morris the guide and Haru the town clerk about the 'far chapter house'.",
 "So there is none. Not on the roads, not in the ledgers. Still, that isn't proof. Not being in the record and not existing are different things. An archivist must know that. …Haru said the Elder straightens his glove when he is thinking hard. Twenty years beside him and I never knew. I thought he was cold. This morning, when I brought up the portraits, he did it three times. I'll call it a family custom a little longer. Only until we're back from the desert."),
"story_4_07a": ("Sirok's Markers",
 "There are marker stones along the canal. Someone raised one for each stretch Sirok dug. The garden-keepers. A line on each. Three are left before the break, buried in sand. Clear them for me. I'll read. And the last handspan has to be dug by hand. Not with a shovel. Sirok had no shovel.",
 "In front of the Cursed Pyramid, clear the sand from three markers with Isabel and join the broken canal.",
 "It's joined. The water crossed. Did you see? A thousand years to cross one handspan. I copied the markers. The garden-keepers spoke to Sirok every day. 'Twenty paces today, well done.' Sirok can't read. They wrote it anyway. Not for anyone to see. Because a day you write down is a day that happened. …That's why I write, too. Fetch water, please. The canal reaches, but filling the basin will take a while. I want it full while I read the king's writing."),
"story_4_10a": ("The Garden Inside",
 "The door is open. A garden no one has seen in a thousand years. The Elder went off to walk alone. Let's go in, the two of us. The king is sitting there, you said. Facing the door. The fountain is dry. Bring one bucket of water. The gatekeeper kept the promise, so this isn't a promise. Just a greeting.",
 "In the garden of the Cursed Pyramid, look at the king's seat and pour water into the dry fountain. (1 water bucket)",
 "When the water pooled, something floated up. A seed. Just one. It must have been wedged in a crack. Will a thousand-year-old seed sprout? Who knows. Let's take it anyway. The writing on the king's knees. Only the last line again. 'When the door opens, to the one who opened it. I could not say that there is no end. You say it.' …Who is that for? Why does reading it make me think of the Elder?"),
"story_4_10b": ("One Seed",
 "That's the seed from the desert king's garden? Wow. Let's plant it! I keep a patch by the road up to the Order. I tend it while I wait for travellers. You can have the sunniest spot. I called Lorenzo too. Nobody is more curious about what will grow.",
 "Plant the garden's seed in Morris the guide's patch.",
 "Planted. I'll water it. Every day. …Can I tell you something odd? Last night the Elder came down this road. Alone. The night you came back from the desert. He stood a long time in front of my patch. In front of an empty row. I asked what he was looking at and he said 'good sun here'. That's the very spot we just planted. And I only offered you that spot a moment ago."),
"story_4_12a": ("The Drawers of Questions",
 "There are drawers before the machine. A whole wall of them. This city asked the machine everything. You punched your question into a card and the machine reckoned an answer. The questions will still be there. The queen's too. Turn them over. See what people were asking by the time they stopped the clocks.",
 "Go through the drawers of questions with Erwin in the Ancient Factory, in order.",
 "It began with the price of wheat. Then wedding days, then whether a son would come home. And then when the end would be. When something that answers is always at hand, people cannot stop asking. They ask even what they must decide themselves. The queen was not a bad queen. She only asked what her people asked. …The bottom drawer was empty, you say. Yes. It would be. The machine was stopped, so whatever was put in never went through. Start the clock and it will. Whatever it holds."),
"story_4_13a": ("Answers a Thousand Years Late",
 "The machine is spitting paper. The answers it owed. A thousand years of them. Gather them up. I… only need one sheet, in that corner. No, never mind. You gather. An old back can't. Don't read what you pick up; give it to me. It isn't fit reading. Dead people's questions.",
 "Gather the answers spilled on the floor of the Ancient Factory in three places.",
 "(The Elder folds the sheets without turning them.) You read them. I said not to. It's all right. Sera's answer was late. A thousand years. Her daughter lived, it said. The mother died not knowing. And the one sheet from the bottom drawer. 'Cannot be reckoned; the entry has been erased.' …Somebody's prank. The paper is recent. Some drifter got in. I'll keep that one. It isn't fit for the record. Don't mention it to Isabel. That child writes everything down."),
"story_4_14a": ("Error",
 "I have the printout. The Elder told you to give it to me, didn't he. How did he look? …Never mind. Sit. This is about us. From the day we stepped onto the dock. The machine reckoned all of it a thousand years ago. But there's a mark at the end of each line. Look with me. I don't want to look alone.",
 "Read the Harbinger's printout with Isabel the archivist.",
 "There are lines marked 'error'. Several. Places where things didn't go as reckoned. The Elder was right. People don't follow the sum. But why was he so angry saying it? People who are right don't get angry. People who hope they're right do. And the last line. 'The one who removes the glove is'. It breaks off there. The machine fell. What came after? A name? …I'll write it down. Broken off, as it is."),
"story_4_17a": ("The Room of Forty Generations",
 "There are rooms behind the slab. Where the priests lived. Forty generations. I want to see what sort of people the six were who took the coin and stopped. Not to curse them. I held a lantern fifteen years. You think there was no day I wanted to stop? Their rooms will tell us why. I'll pick up the coins myself. I won't believe it till I've counted.",
 "Walk through the priests' rooms in the Sunken City with Oscar, in order.",
 "Six coins. One each. A song a family sang for generations, sold for one coin. They didn't sell cheap. They didn't count the price at all. They needed an excuse. They were only waiting for someone to say 'you may stop'. Nobody said it in a thousand years. …The grey cloak knew that. That one knows what people wait for. Do you know why the last two refused? It was on the practice slab. They're brother and sister. If one stops, the other sings alone."),
"story_4_18a": ("Eight Bars",
 "The priest is out of breath. Alone, the gaps between bars can't be filled. Let's fill them. There are three sounding-stones in the temple, where the priests took turns. Sing there and the water carries it down. I'll take the middle. You move from stone to stone and take the bar up. Wrong notes don't matter. Only don't let it break. While the priest catches breath, we ready the Gate.",
 "Carry the song on at the three sounding-stones of the Sunken City, in order.",
 "That's it. The thing that was turning over below has sunk back. For now. The priest says our singing is a 'dialect'. It's quiet because it's listening to something strange. It won't be fooled long. (Oscar starts to laugh and stops.) I held a lantern on the ice fifteen years. Couldn't stop. You ended that for me. My turn now. That song has to be ended for its singer. Let's put it to rest. Properly."),
"story_4_19a": ("Quiet",
 "Oscar brought someone up from the sea! A priest! The last of a family that did nothing but sing for a thousand years! But they won't eat. They've never had anything hot. You can't light a fire under the sea. I've never had a guest like this. I'm making mushroom stew; bring me the makings. It's a first meal. In a thousand years. It has to be good.",
 "Bring 3 red mushrooms, 3 brown mushrooms and 2 bowls to Lorenzo, and sit beside the priest.",
 "They ate! Half a bowl! One spoonful, then sat still a long time and said 'warm'. A word they'd never had cause to say. And all the while they hummed. Those eight bars. Without knowing. It comes out like breath. Mia and the village children heard and started singing along. Skipping rope to it. A thousand years of lullaby turned into a skipping song in half a day. The priest saw and laughed. Oscar says it's the first time he's seen it too."),
"story_4_21a": ("What Was Covered",
 "There's an alcove behind Belas's seat. Where the minutes were kept. At the end of every sitting he sealed the record in wax. Whole. To read it the wax must come off. You do it. My hands… the gloves catch. Don't read aloud what you uncover. Sound stays long in this room.",
 "Peel the wax from the alcove with Erwin in the Ruined Citadel, in order.",
 "You read it. Yes. That was the council. Seven sat six days and none could decide. So they asked the scale. The scale that weighed the kings' disputes. Built on an island apart from the citadel. So that judgment would not look at the kings' faces. What the scale answered would have been in the last case, and that case is gone, you say. …Of all Belas covered, the deepest. Not covered. Removed. Let's go to the eighth seat. See that first."),
"story_4_23a": ("The Island of the Scale",
 "The Elder stopped at the end of the bridge. 'I will not step onto the scale.' Then he sat with his back turned. We'll go ourselves. This was lying where the guardian fell. It looks like an eye, but it's no seal. It's a pathfinder. Throw it and it flies toward the island. The minutes said the question was sent to the scale. What the answer was, what was in the case Belas destroyed, will still be there. The one who answered wouldn't have erased it.",
 "Find the Island of the Scale in the void and walk through the court with Isabel, in order. (Throwing the Eye of Geburah shows the way.)",
 "There is no judgment. The scale couldn't answer. It didn't tip. One name and the world weighed the same. So it sent back 'no judgment', and Belas destroyed that case. Why? With no judgment, the kings had to decide. And deciding made it their crime. …But the fire was divided. So who decided? No line anywhere says anyone did. And the scale is still turning. Still holding the same question after a thousand years. Unable to close the trial, it now tries whatever walks in. Walking. Standing still."),
"story_4_23b": ("How Sins Are Weighed",
 "The archivist told me about the scale. That you mean to put it to rest. Hear me first. I've stood before a thing like that. A man, not a stone. I came back having lost eleven and was questioned. There are places where anything you do is a crime. Run and you fled. Stand and you abandoned them. There's one way out of such a place alive. Hear which crime is being asked about now, and don't do that one thing. Try to keep them all and you'll keep none.",
 "Hear from Captain Roran how to fight before the scale, and bring 8 iron ingots and 2 golden apples.",
 "Good. Summing up. During the fight the scale posts one charge after another. It shows on your screen. Don't jump, don't stand still, don't circle sunwise, things like that. Keep only the one that's posted. Break it and you're marked a sinner and it hits harder. It floats where you can't reach, so watch for when the weapons come down. The fight begins on the Island of the Scale, when you lay a hand on the stand before it. A Memory Gate won't work there. One more thing. (Roran looks at his bootlace. Leaves it.) After the inquiry I was given 'no fault'. That was the hardest part. Don't say that to the scale. It's been unable to say it for a thousand years."),
"story_4_23c": ("The Scale That Will Not Tip",
 "Let's go. Put the scale to rest. I'll be at the wall of judgments. Someone has to close that trial. The ones who asked left a thousand years ago. You close it. I'll write that it's closed. Its name was at the top of the wall. Geburah. An old word for 'severity'.",
 "On the Island of the Scale, lay a hand on the stand to open the trial, and put Geburah to rest.",
 "It's closed. As it stopped, the scale tipped for the first time. Not to either side. Both pans sank together. And a new line was cut at the foot of the wall. Its last judgment. 'Judgment: this question cannot be weighed. Those who asked shall answer.' …It should have written that a thousand years ago. Not 'none' but 'you answer'. The kings pushed it onto the scale, and the scale had no words to hand it back. The Elder is waiting at the bridge. Let's tell him there was no answer. Then who decided? This time I'll be the one to ask. …And when you're back, get some sleep early. We weighed too many heavy things today."),
"story_4_23d": ("At the End of the Bridge",
 "…You closed it. I heard it from here. The pans settling. Isabel asked me: with no judgment, who decided? I didn't answer. It's the first time that child has asked me like that. Without looking away. You want to ask too. Sit. I can't tell all of it. I'll tell as far as I can.",
 "Hear Elder Erwin out.",
 "One left. The Frost Prison. I can't go there. Not even to the door. The child there… the king there knows me. Don't ask why. Ask when you return. I'll answer then. With the gloves off. I promise. (The Elder rises with a hand on the rail. The stone is warm only where he touched it.)"),
"story_4_23e": ("The Road You Walk Alone",
 "Only you go to the Frost Prison. The Elder says he can't, and the archivist follows only as far as the tomb door. I stay here beside the Elder. As eyes. Before you go, walk round the village once. Not an order. It's what's done before an expedition. I left without doing it and lost eleven. Those you never said goodbye to, you can't say it to even when you come back.",
 "See Bram the smith, Oscar the fisher and Morris the guide, then return to Captain Roran.",
 "Done? Good. What did they give you? I won't ask. They gave something. People here don't send a traveller off with empty pockets. Go. Sound carries far over snow. If anything happens I'll hear it. (Roran raises a hand. Doesn't lower it. Not until you've turned the corner.)"),
"story_4_24a": ("Those Who Followed In",
 "There's a line on the wall of every cell. Why they followed. The ice covers it. A torch will melt it. I can't read them all, but I want to read a few. Who they were, the ones who locked themselves in after an eighteen-year-old king. They can't all have been soldiers.",
 "Melt the ice of three cells in the Frosted Prison with torches. (3 torches)",
 "A cook, a drummer boy, a writing master. All wrote the same thing. 'It didn't seem right to leave him alone.' They didn't follow a king. They followed a child. Those are the draugr now. The ones we put to rest. …I came this far hating the kings. The ones who pushed things onto others. This king didn't. But everyone beside him took it on with him. Maybe neither is the answer, pushing it off or carrying it alone. I don't know. I'll only write."),
"story_4_25a": ("The Brother's Crown",
 "The crown rolled to the door and stopped. It shouldn't be left like that. Set it upright at the door. Where the king can see it through the gap. If he calls 'brother?' and no one answers, he should at least know his brother came this far. Something is tucked inside the crown. Paper, I think. Look at that too.",
 "Set the crown upright before the tomb door in the Frosted Prison.",
 "It was a letter. From the brother. Never sent. 'You became king because I said I wouldn't. It should have been me at that council. Then I'd have named the prisoner, I'd have been the one afraid, and you'd have been outside listening to the drum. A thousand years of sorry isn't enough. So I stand at the door.' The brother pushed something off too. The throne, onto his little brother. And then stood at the door a thousand years. …I read it through the gap. After a long while there was a voice. With the spelling of a boy. 'Brother is a fool.' And a little later. 'So am I.'"),
"story_4_27a": ("Before the Order's Door",
 "You're here. The Elder passed an hour ago. He didn't run. He walked. He saw me and bowed his head. First time in thirty years that man has bowed to me. I didn't stop him. It isn't a thing to stop. He went into the archive. Didn't lock it. He's waiting. Before you go in, count what you carry. One who goes to ask should know what they're bringing.",
 "Count what you have gathered with Captain Roran and Isabel the archivist.",
 "Counted? Then go. I stand outside the door. I won't draw. I hope I needn't. That man is the only one who took my side in the inquiry twenty years ago. Whether it was heart or reckoning, I don't want to know today. You find out. (Roran ties his bootlace. Slowly. And doesn't stand even when it's tied.)"),
}
